From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ps4-gladius <ps4-gladius@invalid>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 09:20:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] server: the in-process wake pipe must be non-blocking too.

0742 routed every request for non-blocking mode through pw_set_nonblocking,
because this console refuses fcntl( fd, F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK ) with EACCES. The
wake pipe this patch series adds later still asked with fcntl, and ignored the
answer.

That one is not cosmetic. The main loop drains the pipe with

    while (read( get_unix_fd( fd ), buffer, sizeof(buffer) ) > 0);

which on a blocking descriptor does not end: it reads what the wake-up left,
then waits for a byte nobody will send, with the server thread inside it. The
first timeout to arrive would have hung the server for good -- it has not bitten
yet only because nothing has run long enough to need a timer.
---
diff --git a/server/request.c b/server/request.c
index 014beb18b3..8db8e9a8b8 100644
--- a/server/request.c
+++ b/server/request.c
@@ -1231,8 +1231,8 @@ void open_master_socket(void)
             if (pipe( wake ) == -1) fatal_error( "pipe: %s\n", strerror( errno ));
             fcntl( wake[0], F_SETFD, FD_CLOEXEC );
             fcntl( wake[1], F_SETFD, FD_CLOEXEC );
-            fcntl( wake[0], F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK );
-            fcntl( wake[1], F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK );
+            pw_set_nonblocking( wake[0] );
+            pw_set_nonblocking( wake[1] );
             if (!(wake_fd = create_anonymous_fd( &inprocess_wake_fd_ops, wake[0], &master_socket->obj, 0 )))
                 fatal_error( "out of memory\n" );
             set_fd_events( wake_fd, POLLIN );
